; DOMAIN: mathematics/number-theory/analytic-number-theory [CLOSED, derived 2026-08-01]; Claim: Analytic number theory studies the distribution of primes and arithmetic functions using complex analysis. Key results: Prime Number Theorem (pi(x) ~ x/ln(x)) proved via non-vanishing of zeta(s) on Re(s)=1; Dirichlet's theorem on primes in arithmetic progressions via Dirichlet L-functions; the explicit formula relating prime counting to zeros of zeta; Chebotarev density theorem generalizing Dirichlet; sieve methods (Eratosthenes, Selberg, large sieve) bounding prime counts; the circle method for additive problems (Goldbach, Waring); modular forms and their Fourier coefficients encoding arithmetic data; the Riemann hypothesis equivalent to optimal error term in PNT. Bridges to zeta_function (zero distribution, explicit formulae), automorphic_forms (modular form coefficients, trace formula), and class_field_theory (Chebotarev density).;; Prime Number Theorem: prime counting function pi(x) is asymptotic to x/ln(x), proved via non-vanishing of zeta(s) on Re(s)=1
(|- ((==> (&& (--> PrimeCountingFunction $pi) (--> Variable $x) (--> TendsTo (Ratio $pi $x (Divide $x (Log $x))) 1))) (--> PrimeNumberTheorem $pi)) (stv 1.0 0.9))
;; Dirichlet theorem on primes in AP: for coprime a,q, infinitely many primes p ≡ a (mod q), proved via non-vanishing of L(s,chi) on Re(s)=1
(|- ((==> (&& (--> Integer $a) (--> Integer $q) (--> Coprime $a $q) (--> DirichletCharacter $chi) (--> NonVanishing (Line (RealPart $s) 1)))) (--> InfinitelyManyPrimes $p (Congruent $p $a $q))) (stv 1.0 0.9))
;; Explicit formula: relates sum over prime powers to sum over zeros of zeta, connecting prime distribution to zero locations
(|- ((==> (&& (--> PrimeCountingFunction $pi) (--> Function $zeta) (--> SumOver $rho (NonTrivialZeros $zeta)) (--> SumOver $p (PrimePowers $p $k)) (--> Relates $pi $rho))) (--> ExplicitFormula $pi $zeta)) (stv 1.0 0.9))
;; Chebotarev density theorem: generalizes Dirichlet — Frobenius conjugacy classes in Galois group have natural density proportional to class size
(|- ((==> (&& (--> GaloisExtension $L $K) (--> ConjugacyClass $C (GaloisGroup $L $K)) (--> Density $d (SetOf $p (FrobeniusIn $C $p))) (--> Equals $d (Ratio (Size $C) (Size (GaloisGroup $L $K)))))) (--> ChebotarevDensity $L $K $C)) (stv 1.0 0.9))
;; Sieve methods: combinatorial bounds on prime counts using inclusion-exclusion, includes Selberg sieve and large sieve
(|- ((==> (&& (--> SieveMethod $S) (--> SetOf $A) (--> InclusionExclusionBound $S $A) (--> UpperBound (Count (PrimesIn $A $S))))) (--> SieveTheory $S $A)) (stv 1.0 0.9))
;; Circle method: decomposes additive problems via Fourier analysis on the unit circle, used for Goldbach and Waring problems
(|- ((==> (&& (--> AdditiveProblem $P) (--> MajorArcs $M) (--> MinorArcs $m) (--> FourierDecomposition $P $M $m) (--> Estimate $P (MinorArcBound $m)))) (--> CircleMethod $P)) (stv 1.0 0.9))
;; Modular form coefficients: Fourier coefficients of modular forms encode multiplicative arithmetic data, Hecke eigenvalues give Euler products
(|- ((==> (&& (--> ModularForm $f $k) (--> FourierCoefficient $a_n (Apply $f)) (--> Multiplicative $a_n) (--> HeckeEigenvalue $lambda (Apply $T_n $f)))) (--> ModularFormCoefficients $f $a_n)) (stv 1.0 0.9))
